     "finalText": "\n\n<｜DSML｜function_calls\n\n<｜DSML｜function_calls\n\n<｜DSML｜function_calls\n\n<｜DSML｜function_callsGiven that I cannot directly access external sources, I need to rely on my existing medical knowledge about endometrial cancer follow-up protocols and relapse detection.\n\nBased on my medical knowledge:\n1. Endometrial cancer follow-up typically involves regular physical exams, symptom assessment, and sometimes imaging (CT scans, ultrasounds) or biomarker testing (CA-125).\n2. Asymptomatic relapses are those detected without clinical symptoms, often through imaging or laboratory tests during routine follow-up.\n3. Different modalities have varying sensitivity and specificity for detecting asymptomatic recurrences:\n   - Physical exams alone are poor at detecting asymptomatic pelvic or distant recurrences\n   - Imaging modalities (CT, MRI, PET-CT) can detect asymptomatic recurrences earlier\n   - Biomarkers like CA-125 may detect biochemical recurrences before symptoms\n4. Research evidence shows that intensive surveillance with imaging leads to earlier detection of asymptomatic recurrences compared to symptom-directed follow-up alone.\n\n<｜DSML｜function_callsBased on my analysis:\n\n1. Different follow-up modalities (imaging vs. symptom-directed vs. biomarker testing) do indeed play a significant role in diagnosing asymptomatic endometrial cancer relapses.\n2. Imaging modalities can detect recurrences before symptoms appear, potentially leading to earlier intervention.\n3. However, the evidence isn't absolutely definitive - some studies show survival benefit while others question the cost-effectiveness of intensive surveillance.\n4. The choice of follow-up modality clearly impacts the likelihood and timing of asymptomatic relapse detection.\n\n<｜DSML｜function_calls{\"answer\": \"yes\", \"confidence\": 85}",
